Z||000018||Oreif||09-12-2001||01:22 AM||||Gas in Indiana and central/southern Illinois has increased to ove $3.00 per gallon. On my way to work tonight the Mobil stations had gas at $1.82. When I drove home tonight they LOWERED the price to $1.79. (I just filled up) It seems that Exxon has announced they will NOT raise prices until there is a confirmed shortage. BP followed shortly after. There are still gas prices rising per local news.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">    <BR><B><I>Got Emblems?</I></B>||||reg||1
Z||000019||fierospeeder||09-12-2001||03:40 AM||||On the news in illinois, it was said that, central and southern illinois is increasing the price for gas.  aka gouging.<P>Several investigators are going to go to those gas stations that are increasing the price to $5 a gallon.<P>If you know any gas station that is doing that, report them to your state attorney.||||reg||1
Z||000020||JetroGT||09-12-2001||08:25 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by EdsB52:<BR><B>There's one way to get it for free, forever.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Are you talking about making your own gas out of potatoes or apples? <BR>really, I'm being searious.||||reg||1
Z||000021||kslish||09-12-2001||08:55 AM||||Stopped by the local station this morning on my way to work. Still the same $1.48/gallon it's been all week.  The stations that I passed were all still similarly priced.  <P>After all, we had one of the hijacked planes go down 40 miles from where I am....if anyone should be panicking, it's us....<P>The only thing that might raise gas prices on a local basis is if people start panicking and hoard gas causing local station shortages before the next scheduled shipment arrives.  Like the president of BP said in an interview...the refineries are still producing gas, so there should be no concern over fuel in the forseeable future so far.  Oil prices only rose $4 to $29 a barrel in the aftermath which really isn't that high in comparison to how it was during the Gulf War (around $32/gallon).  And it takes crude oil prices a month or two to really filter down into the refined gasoline prices.<P>Ken S.||||reg||1
Z||000022||Wolfhound||09-12-2001||09:23 AM||||If you are forced to pay a unrealistic price for gas make sure you get a written receipt. It can be used as evidence. Price gouging is a crime.||||reg||1
Z||000023||Fformula88||09-12-2001||10:06 AM||||$5-$6 a gallon  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"><P>Prices this morning are the same $1.41 that they have been for the last week or so around here.  When I get home from work... who knows!  <P>------------------<BR>John  <BR>AOLIM: Fformula88<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero2m6siga.JPG">   <BR>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000024||Tigger||09-12-2001||10:33 AM||||All day yesterday people were lining up all around gas stations even sitting in the streets waiting to pay $1.89 or more for gas.  They were sitting in LINES long enough to be sitting in the street?  The price had jumped about $.30 to .40 by the afternoon and it was very interesting how many stations went to $1.89 a gallon.<P>It's really a wonder they didn't raise prices even more since people were willing to fill up their tanks no matter what the price was just to feel "secure."<P>I agree the gouging is just not right.  The fear of even more gouging is what forced people to the pump.  This is where we need to take a stand and boycott the gas stations that gouged people $2.00 or more yesterday.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 09-12-2001).]||||reg||13
